WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.944
The Hispaniola was far out in the bay, but at last, our small boat came alongside the ship and we stepped aboard.

2
00:00:07.945 --> 00:00:17.220
The first mate, Mr. Arrow, a brown old sailor with earrings in his ears, saluted us, and the squire greeted him like an old friend.

3
00:00:17.221 --> 00:00:24.070
But I soon observed that things were not the same between Mr. Trelawney and Captain Smollett.

4
00:00:24.071 --> 00:00:31.336
We’d hardly gone below deck and entered the captain’s cabin when the captain himself came in and angrily shut the door behind him.

5
00:00:31.337 --> 00:00:37.109
"Captain Smollett, all is well, I hope?" Squire Trelawney said.

6
00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:45.779
"Well, sir," the captain said, "I’ll be frank. I don’t like this voyage, I don’t like the men, and I don’t like my first mate."

7
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.983
"Perhaps, sir, you don’t like the ship?" the squire asked angrily.

8
00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:54.862
"I can’t say that, as I haven’t tried her," the captain replied.

9
00:00:54.863 --> 00:00:59.452
"I guess you don’t like your employer either?" the squire shot back.

10
00:00:59.453 --> 00:01:03.910
Seeing a fight brewing, Dr. Livesey cut in. "Calm down, gentlemen.

11
00:01:03.911 --> 00:01:08.556
Questions like that can only produce ill feelings, Squire," he said.

12
00:01:08.557 --> 00:01:13.274
"Captain, I’d like an explanation. Why don’t you like this voyage?"

13
00:01:13.275 --> 00:01:19.102
"I’ve just learned we’re going after treasure—heard it from my own crew," the captain said. "Searching

14
00:01:19.103 --> 00:01:27.102
for buried treasure can be dangerous, especially when the reason for the voyage is kept secret from the captain, Mr. Trelawney." "What

15
00:01:27.103 --> 00:01:32.525
are you suggesting, sir?" Trelawney asked indignantly. "There’s

16
00:01:32.526 --> 00:01:35.785
been too much talk," the captain said.

17
00:01:35.786 --> 00:01:41.229
"I don’t believe either of you know what we’re facing, but I’ll tell you what I think will happen—it’ll

18
00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:45.582
come down to life or death, and a narrow escape, if we’re lucky." "We

19
00:01:45.583 --> 00:01:49.263
are not as ignorant as you believe," Dr. Livesey said. "You

20
00:01:49.264 --> 00:01:53.175
say you don’t like the crew. Aren’t they good seamen?" "I

21
00:01:53.176 --> 00:01:58.624
should have been allowed to choose my own crew, sir," Captain Smollett replied. "Perhaps

22
00:01:58.625 --> 00:02:03.080
my friend should have included you in the hiring," said the doctor. "But

23
00:02:03.081 --> 00:02:08.819
the squire did not mean to overstep his authority. You don’t like Mr. Arrow?" "I

24
00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.788
don’t, sir. I believe he’s a good seaman, but he’s too chummy with the men. A

25
00:02:13.789 --> 00:02:18.854
first mate should behave with authority. Mr. Arrow acts as if he’s one of the crew." "Tell

26
00:02:18.855 --> 00:02:22.370
us what you want, Captain," the doctor said. "If

27
00:02:22.371 --> 00:02:29.831
you’re determined to go on this voyage," Smollett said, "then all the gunpowder and arms must be stored under my cabin. Second,

28
00:02:29.832 --> 00:02:34.351
I want to move your berths right beside my cabin." "Anything

29
00:02:34.352 --> 00:02:37.730
else?" asked the squire. "There’s

30
00:02:37.731 --> 00:02:41.117
been too much blabbing," said the captain. "Far

31
00:02:41.118 --> 00:02:44.158
too much," the doctor agreed. "I’ve

32
00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:50.014
heard you have a map of an island, with crosses that show where the treasure is," the captain said, and

33
00:02:50.015 --> 00:02:55.017
then he named the exact latitude and longitude of the island. "I

34
00:02:55.018 --> 00:02:59.186
never told a soul!" Squire Trelawney cried. "The

35
00:02:59.187 --> 00:03:03.014
crew knows all about the island, sir," said the captain. "Livesey,

36
00:03:03.015 --> 00:03:07.763
you or Hawkins must have said something," the squire said. "It

37
00:03:07.764 --> 00:03:11.466
doesn’t matter who," replied the doctor. "Well,

38
00:03:11.467 --> 00:03:21.998
gentlemen," the captain said, "I don’t know who has this map, but keep it secret, even from me and Mr. Arrow. Otherwise, I will resign." "You

39
00:03:21.999 --> 00:03:28.327
fear a mutiny," the doctor said. "That’s the reason you want all of us and the guns in the stern of the ship." "I

40
00:03:28.328 --> 00:03:33.402
wouldn’t go to sea if I had evidence of a possible mutiny," Captain Smollett said. "No

41
00:03:33.403 --> 00:03:38.541
captain would do that. But I am responsible for the life of everyone on board. So

42
00:03:38.542 --> 00:03:44.014
I’m asking you to take certain precautions or let me resign, that’s all." "I

43
00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:50.185
will do as you desire," said the squire, "but I don’t like your attitude very much." "I

44
00:03:50.186 --> 00:03:57.531
don’t care what you think, sir," the captain said. "But I must do my duty." And with that, he left. "Trelawney,"

45
00:03:57.532 --> 00:04:07.360
the doctor said, "I believe you’ve managed to get two honest men on board with you—that man and Long John Silver." "Of

46
00:04:07.361 --> 00:04:17.671
course Silver is honest," the squire yelled, "but as for that intolerable captain, I think he is unmanly and downright unpatriotic." "Well,"

47
00:04:17.672 --> 00:04:21.897
the doctor said, "we shall see." When

48
00:04:21.898 --> 00:04:28.973
we came back on deck, the men were already moving the arms and gunpowder into the space underneath the cabin. The

49
00:04:28.974 --> 00:04:35.305
captain and Mr. Arrow supervised while the men worked hard to fix up the whole ship.

50
00:04:35.306 --> 00:04:43.706
Six new berths had been constructed in the stern of the ship from part of the galley, with a passageway to the captain’s cabin on the port side.

51
00:04:43.707 --> 00:04:50.415
It was decided that the doctor, the squire, Redruth, Hunter, Joyce, and I would sleep there.

52
00:04:50.416 --> 00:04:54.270
Mr. Arrow would sleep on deck in a hammock to keep watch.

53
00:04:54.271 --> 00:05:01.121
As the crew finished their work, Long John and a few men came alongside the ship in a shore boat.

54
00:05:01.122 --> 00:05:08.913
Long John scrambled up the side of the ship like a monkey and called out, "Yo ho, mates! What’s going on?"

55
00:05:08.914 --> 00:05:12.430
"We’re moving all the guns," a man answered.

56
00:05:12.431 --> 00:05:18.966
"Why?" Long John bellowed. "If we do that, it will delay our departure!"

57
00:05:18.967 --> 00:05:25.777
"It’s my orders!" snapped the captain. "You may go below, cook. The men will want supper."

58
00:05:25.778 --> 00:05:31.941
"Aye, aye, sir," Long John answered, and saluting, he disappeared toward the galley.

59
00:05:31.942 --> 00:05:35.115
"He’s a good man, Captain," the doctor said.

60
00:05:35.116 --> 00:05:44.283
"Very likely, sir," Captain Smollett replied. "Easy with that, men—easy," he said to the men carrying the crates of gunpowder.

61
00:05:44.284 --> 00:05:47.972
Then he saw me examining a small cannon on deck.

62
00:05:47.973 --> 00:05:54.761
"Hey, you! Ship’s boy!" he shouted. "Leave that alone! Go to the cook and get to work."

63
00:05:54.762 --> 00:06:01.399
As I was hurrying off, I heard him say quite loudly to the doctor, "I’ll have no favorites on my ship."

64
00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:08.879
I fully agreed with the squire. I hated the captain deeply.

